{"kind":"artifact","text":"Implementation report for repair child bc84e256 (parent df17d2b6 via 9962a1b8, landing-obligation I1) at exact head a0741f486012f7a0cfec753f5a05811760a67b7e on request/landing-obligation-i1, four commits over main be3b069c, 32 changed paths; supersedes the report 4b869dbd at 605163a3 after planner verdict 6b0058a8 (ratified). The mixed-target hold-authority defect is repaired under one invariant, stated once in architecture.md: a request inherits a hold, and its owner, only from an ancestor that resolves to the destination it inherited. The one depth-eight walk resolves the destination from the nearest value triple, then admits held ancestors only where their resolved repository and ref equal the selected destination (target_head is a measurement, not identity); held ancestors are collected as met and filtered after selection so a nearer irrelevant hold cannot hide the relevant one; among survivors the nearest governs and equally near ones naming different owners refuse with conflicting hold ownership in target ancestry; restate the hold. Planner seed TestReviewHoldCannotComeFromAnUnselectedTargetBranch fails at 605163a3 and passes here, folded into landing_test.go as TestAHoldComesOnlyFromTheSelectedDestination with five cases: the counterexample, reversed citation order, other-branch owner refused, selected destination owner admitted, and both branches owing one destination where the nearer hold governs. A never-reachable nearest-first comparison in the selection loop is removed because the queue is walked in nondecreasing depth. Nearest-value-triple selection, depth bound, no-artifact barrier and traversal through pre-state@3 requests are untouched. Earlier rounds stand: state@3 result choice with hardened target_ref, bounded inheritance, hold and release authorship through readReleaseClaim, explicit no-artifact completion, awaiting-review/awaiting-authorization/awaiting-landing replacing awaiting-merge with no alias across all enumerat